`k8s_events` (lib/MCP/K8s.pm:1385-1395) formats the listed Events through `_format_list` -> `_format_resource_summary` (lib/MCP/K8s.pm:582-644). That summary only reads `metadata`, `kind`, `status` and `spec`. A core/v1 `Event` has neither `spec` nor `status`; everything that makes an event useful (`reason`, `message`, `type`, `count`, `involvedObject`, `lastTimestamp`) is top-level and never copied.

Reproduce:

```
perl -Ilib -MMCP::K8s -MIO::K8s -e 'my $k=MCP::K8s->new(namespaces=>["default"]); my $ev=IO::K8s->new->struct_to_object("Event",{metadata=>{name=>"web.17a",namespace=>"default"},involvedObject=>{kind=>"Pod",name=>"web"},reason=>"BackOff",message=>"Back-off restarting failed container",type=>"Warning",count=>7}); print $k->_to_json($k->_format_resource_summary($ev))'
```

prints only `{"kind":"Event","name":"web.17a","namespace":"default"}`. The tool is advertised as 'Get Kubernetes events for debugging' and the LLM gets a list of opaque event names. `k8s_list resource=Event` has the same problem. t/05 only checks the selector/permission plumbing, not the content.

Done when: an event summary carries at least type, reason, message, count, involvedObject (kind/name) and last/first timestamp (events.k8s.io fields `note`/`regarding` if that class is used), with a test asserting the message text reaches the tool output.

